{"chunk_id": "45157032302d", "text": "In this section, the Pandavas stay with King Drupada after the revelation of their identities. Drupada initially proposes that Arjuna formally marry his daughter, Draupadi. However, Yudhishthira announces an unexpected decision: Draupadi will become the wife of all five Pandava brothers, following their mother Kunti\u2019s command and their custom of sharing whatever they obtain. Drupada and his son Dhrishtadyumna object, arguing that polyandry contradicts tradition, morality, and the Vedas. As the debate intensifies, the sage Vyasa arrives. Drupada seeks his judgment, while each participant expresses uncertainty about the subtle nature of dharma. Yudhishthira defends the proposal by citing ancient precedents and emphasizing obedience to one\u2019s mother. The episode highlights a central Mahabharata theme: dharma is complex, contextual, and often disputed even among the wise.\nDebate arises over Draupadi marrying all five Pandava brothers; Yudhishthira defends it while Drupada and others question its morality until Vyasa arrives.\nThemes: polyandry debate, dharma and moral ambiguity, maternal authority, marriage customs, Vyasa's intervention", "vector": [-0.00723238242790103, -0.026237474754452705, -0.009073960594832897, -0.005732332356274128, -0.01238210592418909, 0.026237474754452705, -0.020197097212076187, 0.01328615378588438, -0.019326532259583473, -0.03634941577911377, 0.009777109138667583, 0.008136429823935032, 0.011163315735757351, 0.01076821330934763, -0.01662108674645424, 0.01384867262095213, 0.037447668612003326, -0.004536980297416449, 0.026920534670352936, -0.026411589235067368, 0.0007596512441523373, -0.016527332365512848, -0.00557161308825016, 0.025527630001306534, -0.025058865547180176, 0.047171205282211304, 0.017143424600362778, -0.02350524254143238, 0.004138529766350985, 0.0011476384242996573, -0.014357618056237698, -0.0033985499758273363, 0.018281854689121246, -0.01664787158370018, -0.027014287188649178, -0.003